cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Governance Concentration Beyond Token Allocation: An Institutional Design Analysis of DePIN and DeFi"
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite as follows."
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: "Zukowski"
    given-names: "Zach"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3642-2450"
    email: "zach@tokenization.systems"
    affiliation: "Tokenization Systems"
abstract: >-
  A 40-protocol cross-section documents that initial token allocation design
  does not predict post-distribution governance concentration (r = 0.19,
  p = 0.25, N = 37). Protocols with generous community distributions show
  concentration comparable to those with heavy insider allocations. Sector
  membership predicts concentration (DePIN more concentrated than DeFi,
  Mann-Whitney p = 0.031, Cohen's d = 0.96) after adjusting for protocol
  age, valuation, and insider allocation. The exclusion methodology
  identifies 69 addresses controlled by protocols themselves that appear
  on holder lists but cannot vote; correcting for these changes affected
  protocols' HHI by up to 5x.
keywords:
  - tokenomics
  - institutional design
  - governance concentration
  - DAO governance
  - DePIN
  - delegation amplification
  - Herfindahl-Hirschman Index
  - political philosophy of institutions
  - polycentric governance
  - exclusion methodology
